f 17592186045417] [17592186051017 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186051019 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051017 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186064322 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051017 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186064323 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051017 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186064324 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051017 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186064341 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051017 #edb/ref 1031 "Factoring Polynomials Using GCFs" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051017 #edb/ref 1033 353 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051017 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "7a611e07-341d-5ee4-914f-fa436e11a160" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083040 #edb/ref 1069 "e-5822" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083040 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve the equation $\\dfrac{3}{u+1} - \\dfrac{1}{3u} =\\dfrac{5}{u^2 +u}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083040 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ec516fb4-a497-579c-b19d-ebc0a081eb58" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083040 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we factor the quadratic denominator, giving us the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{u+1} - \\dfrac{1}{3u}  =\\dfrac{5}{u(u+1)}.\n$$\n\nWe now make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{LCM}\\big( 3u, \\:u+1, \\: u(u+1) \\big) = 3u(u+1).\n$$\n\n- The values $u=-1$ and $u=0$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{u+1}-\\frac{1}{3u} & =\\frac{5}{u(u+1)} \\\\ 3u(u+1)\\cdot (\\frac{3}{u+1}-\\frac{1}{3u}) & =3u(u+1)\\cdot \\frac{5}{u(u+1)} \\\\ \\frac{9u(u+1)}{u+1}-\\frac{3u(u+1)}{3u} & =\\frac{15u(u+1)}{u(u+1)} \\\\ \\frac{9u\\cdot \\cancel{(u+1)}}{\\cancel{u+1}}-\\frac{\\cancel{3u}\\cdot (u+1)}{\\cancel{3u}} & =\\frac{15\\cdot \\cancel{u(u+1)}}{\\cancel{u(u+1)}} \\\\ 9u-(u+1) & =15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $u,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9u-(u+1) & =15 \\\\ 9u-u-1 & =15 \\\\ 8u & =16 \\\\ u & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $2\\neq -1$ and $2\\neq 0,$ we conclude that $u=2$ is a valid solution. \n\nTherefore, the solution to our original equation is $u=2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083041 #edb/ref 1069 "e-5824" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083041 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve the equation $\\dfrac{3}{k-3} -\\dfrac {2}{k-2}  = \\dfrac{2}{k^2-5k+6}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083041 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a578b895-ea0e-5d83-aa1d-04a2fe15f332" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083041 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we factor the quadratic denominator, giving us the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{k-3} -\\dfrac {2}{k-2}  = \\dfrac{2}{(k-2)(k-3)}.\n$$\n\nWe now make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{LCM}\\big( k-2, k-3, (k-2)(k-3) \\big) = (k-2)(k-3).\n$$\n\n- The values $k=2$ and $k=3$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal to zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{k-3}-\\frac{2}{k-2} & =\\frac{2}{(k-2)(k-3)} \\\\ (k-2)(k-3)\\cdot (\\frac{3}{k-3}-\\frac{2}{k-2}) & =(k-2)(k-3)\\cdot \\frac{2}{(k-2)(k-3)} \\\\ \\frac{3(k-2)(k-3)}{k-3}-\\frac{2(k-2)(k-3)}{k-2} & =\\frac{2(k-2)(k-3)}{(k-2)(k-3)} \\\\ \\frac{3(k-2)\\cdot \\cancel{(k-3)}}{\\cancel{k-3}}-\\frac{2\\cdot \\cancel{(k-2)}\\cdot (k-3)}{\\cancel{k-2}} & =\\frac{2\\cdot \\cancel{(k-2)(k-3)}}{\\cancel{(k-2)(k-3)}} \\\\ 3(k-2)-2(k-3) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $k,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(k-2)-2(k-3) & =2 \\\\ 3k-6-2k+6 & =2 \\\\ k & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, since $k=2$ is not a valid solution, our equation has no solutions." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083136 #edb/ref 1069 "e-9367" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083136 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve the equation $\\dfrac{4} {x - 2} - \\dfrac{8} {x(x - 2)} = \\dfrac {3} {x}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083136 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3384b27a-d977-5421-800e-64a3581b939d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083136 #edb/ref 1074 "We make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{LCM}\\big(x-2,\\: x(x-2),\\: x \\big) = x(x-2).\n$$\n\n- The values $x = 0$ and $x=2$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{x-2}-\\frac{8}{x(x-2)} & =\\frac{3}{x} \\\\ x(x-2)\\cdot (\\frac{4}{x-2}-\\frac{8}{x(x-2)}) & =x(x-2)\\cdot \\frac{3}{x} \\\\ \\frac{4x(x-2)}{x-2}-\\frac{8x(x-2)}{x(x-2)} & =\\frac{3x(x-2)}{x} \\\\ \\frac{4x\\cancel{(x-2)}}{\\cancel{x-2}}-\\frac{8\\cancel{x(x-2)}}{\\cancel{x(x-2)}} & =\\frac{3\\cancel{x}(x-2)}{\\cancel{x}} \\\\ 4x-8 & =3(x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $x,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x-8 & =3(x-2) \\\\ 4x-8 & =3x-6 \\\\ 4x-3x & =-6+8 \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, since $x=2$ is not a valid solution, we conclude that our equation has no solutions." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083241 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186051017 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083241 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186083494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083241 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186083538 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083241 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186253379 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083241 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186253380 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083241 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186253456 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083241 #