#edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186084151 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083412 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186084286 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083412 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186084287 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083412 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083412 #edb/ref 1045 "Advanced Rational Equations" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083412 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186084287 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083412 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050211 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083412 #edb/ref 1050 720.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083412 #edb/ref 1050 720.0 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186083412 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "7c5c3245-5a5b-577b-a9ca-fd2370c9f2ee" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084149 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186084151 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084149 #edb/ref 1061 21052 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084149 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5840fe27-c9ee-5863-82d0-ba71f16f50ef" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084149 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186084459 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084150 #edb/ref 1061 21053 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084150 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1cefe61a-e363-5e65-8b3f-4f1e372a0f50" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084150 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186083241 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084151 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186084150 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084151 #edb/ref 1061 21055 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084151 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ef795b4c-168e-5096-b306-7b42c4f166c5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084151 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186083242 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084286 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186084149 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084286 #edb/ref 1061 35068 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084286 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d95d199e-d11b-55e9-a721-56d20ee34fe7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084286 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186083334 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084287 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186084286 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084287 #edb/ref 1061 35079 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084287 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8ead11f6-987c-50c1-8754-c7518e6c4b62" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084287 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186084489 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084459 #edb/ref 1064 "When a rational equation contains a quadratic denominator, it's often helpful to factor this denominator first. By doing so, we may spot the least common denominator of the equation, leading to a more straightforward solution. \n\nFor example, consider the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{x-1}+\\dfrac{2}{x-2}=\\dfrac{5}{x^2-3x+2}\n$$\n\nNotice that the right-hand side of our equation contains a term with a quadratic denominator. This denominator can be factored, giving us the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{x-1}+\\dfrac{2}{x-2}=\\dfrac{5}{(x-1)(x-2)}\\,.\n$$\n\nWe now make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{LCM}(x-1,\\,x-2,\\,(x-1)(x-2))=(x-1)(x-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The values $x=1$ and $x=2$ *cannot* be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)(x-2)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{x-1}+\\frac{2}{x-2}) & =(x-1)(x-2)\\cdot (\\frac{5}{(x-1)(x-2)}) \\\\ \\frac{(x-1)(x-2)}{x-1}+\\frac{2(x-1)(x-2)}{x-2} & =\\frac{5(x-1)(x-2)}{(x-1)(x-2)} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{(x-1)}\\cdot (x-2)}{\\cancel{x-1}}+\\frac{2(x-1)\\cdot \\cancel{(x-2)}}{\\cancel{x-2}} & =\\frac{5\\cancel{(x-1)(x-2)}}{\\cancel{(x-1)(x-2)}} \\\\ (x-2)+2(x-1) & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $x,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)+2(x-1) & =5 \\\\ x-2+2x-2 & =5 \\\\ 3x-4 & =5 \\\\ 3x & =9 \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $3\\neq 1$ and $3\\neq 2,$ we conclude that $x=3$ is a valid solution. \n\nTherefore, the solution to our original equation is $x=3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084459 #edb/ref 1065 3763 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084459 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "8acc5c11-cb6c-5f97-94fb-077195817e1e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084459 #edb/ref 1067 "Solving Rational Equations by Factoring" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084489 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we want to solve the following rational equation. \n\n![](images/c5/c5b84db354c24a0b6f4c45ecc857668a6514c51161867b6797a37acea25d0bd1.svg)\n\nNotice that each of the denominators in our equation is either a binomial or product of binomials. \n\nFrom here, we make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator (![](images/22/22a645a9e1c8260201c66c6af84cdf1c9d06b534716a2e31be23d8f6e744f5cb.svg)) is the least common multiple (![](images/2d/2db8412ff4edf9f090c02dda4c50cd1562b84f6ae99cb74f75750881a3ca2aa3.svg)) of the denominators, which in this case is given by\n![](images/47/47cf83f34dba1af580d8d103f4b744888633f83657f127b148a717f7bba7b392.svg)\n\n- The values ![](images/4a/4ac5ad4e585fa76e28a628e40b8284f2cf3127d51053b40815b9d06cd5e28afa.svg) and ![](images/d4/d463819d45d8f511fb456fbbcf014015220fb127acab0d427d1954e5674f4620.svg) cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n![](images/7b/7b8b7376a449d72d6560bc2ee8f29836c346e35ddd8b282468aa8190c852e3c0.svg)\n\nAfter canceling, we are left with the equation\n\n![](images/36/367bb9f537cc6d99f4d5a92953119e2fd57d23a9851ab0a7d32af68902314da3.svg)\n\nSolving this equation for ![](images/f6/f6ea97a79535415eaed351835491b6719dba8181044eed7f94e41feb7bcac8d6.svg) we get the following:\n\n![](images/27/27ff1b0ed70f265f4c39f32116b2d019cbb514c8f27a43fbd7e8e8e1fb4d2ffa.svg)\n\nSo, ![](images/60/600957eb8a00416e73e77c1866e0c065b489f6d32faa3215f3cef167ad8fcc21.svg) is a *potential* solution. \n\nWe now need to check this value against the prohibited solutions ![](images/4a/4ac5ad4e585fa76e28a628e40b8284f2cf3127d51053b40815b9d06cd5e28afa.svg) and ![](images/d4/d463819d45d8f511fb456fbbcf014015220fb127acab0d427d1954e5674f4620.svg) mentioned earlier. Now, since ![](images/94/948de5597ce1e4ad93b6d3344b463694d2cf1780903ca30a916a784d672e4d7c.svg) and ![](images/fa/fa8945e1ccf216ac0b94b3ae625f4c284190630782a4c0c2e24f4a94ea520d40.svg) we have that ![](images/60/600957eb8a00416e73e77c1866e0c065b489f6d32faa3215f3cef167ad8fcc21.svg) is indeed a valid solution.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the solution to our original equation is ![](images/09/0981bec86ce7424eb7dabbaa3827cc7591360dc24997730f3773c002f72a673b.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084489 #edb/ref 1065 5712 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084489 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "bbec5a32-ab56-51ba-8362-e120d00857da" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084489 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186250620 #edb/ref 1080 "v=-3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186250620 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186250620 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0801c677-febc-5048-b4a9-bd16e29096aa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186250878 #edb/ref 1080 "v=\\frac{1}{4}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186250878 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186250878 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "259a4b59-97e1-5b2a-ae19-ec0054e36543" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186251051 #edb/ref 1080 "No solutions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186251051 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186251051 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3ba949de-81f6-54a0-8f19-c9fed59db56e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186251461 #edb/ref 1080 "No solutions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186251461 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186251461 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "6f6